1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,233
<font color="#FFFFFF">Fine-tuning large language models normally requires
backpropagation,</font>

2
00:00:05,313 --> 00:00:10,047
<font color="#FFFFFF">which stores activations and gradients and consumes
enormous memory.</font>

3
00:00:10,127 --> 00:00:13,940
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper, from Princeton University, introduces MeZO,</font>

4
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:21,018
<font color="#FFFFFF">a memory-efficient zeroth-order optimizer that
fine-tunes language models using only forward passes.</font>

5
00:00:21,098 --> 00:00:25,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">MeZO adapts the classical zeroth-order SGD method
to run in place,</font>

6
00:00:25,770 --> 00:00:29,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">so training costs the same memory as inference.</font>

7
00:00:29,097 --> 00:00:34,538
<font color="#FFFFFF">On a single 80-gigabyte A100 GPU, MeZO can train a
30-billion-parameter model,</font>

8
00:00:34,618 --> 00:00:38,785
<font color="#FFFFFF">where backpropagation with Adam fits only a
2.7-billion one.</font>

9
00:00:38,865 --> 00:00:41,263
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across many tasks and model scales,</font>

10
00:00:41,343 --> 00:00:46,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">MeZO matches fine-tuning performance with up to
twelve times less memory.</font>

11
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:50,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">Fine-tuning has driven much of the recent success
of language models,</font>

12
00:00:50,911 --> 00:00:54,932
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it comes at a steep memory cost.</font>

13
00:00:55,012 --> 00:00:59,033
<font color="#FFFFFF">Backpropagation must cache intermediate activations
and store gradients and optimizer states,</font>

14
00:00:59,113 --> 00:01:03,134
<font color="#FFFFFF">which together can require up to twelve times the
memory of plain inference.</font>

15
00:01:03,214 --> 00:01:07,236
<font color="#FFFFFF">As models scale into the tens of billions of parameters,</font>

16
00:01:07,316 --> 00:01:11,337
<font color="#FFFFFF">this becomes the binding constraint.</font>

17
00:01:11,417 --> 00:01:15,438
<font color="#FFFFFF">Concretely,</font>

18
00:01:15,518 --> 00:01:19,539
<font color="#FFFFFF">a single eighty-gigabyte A100 GPU can run inference
on a thirty-billion-parameter model,</font>

19
00:01:19,619 --> 00:01:23,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet standard Adam fine-tuning on the same hardware
is limited to only a two-point-seven-billion-parameter model.</font>

20
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:30,913
<font color="#FFFFFF">Zeroth-order optimization offers a tempting escape:
it can estimate a gradient using only two forward passes,</font>

21
00:01:30,993 --> 00:01:33,216
<font color="#FFFFFF">requiring no backpropagation at all.</font>

22
00:01:33,296 --> 00:01:40,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">The catch is that classical analyses suggest
zeroth-order methods converge catastrophically slowly for large models,</font>

23
00:01:40,716 --> 00:01:44,858
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the rate degrading in proportion to the number
of parameters.</font>

24
00:01:44,938 --> 00:01:49,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">That pessimism, combined with a naive
implementation that still doubles memory,</font>

25
00:01:49,992 --> 00:01:54,773
<font color="#FFFFFF">is why zeroth-order methods have been overlooked
for modern language models.</font>

26
00:01:54,853 --> 00:02:01,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper asks whether that pessimism actually
holds when fine-tuning pre-trained models on downstream tasks.</font>

27
00:02:02,190 --> 00:02:06,059
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes four main contributions.</font>

28
00:02:06,139 --> 00:02:10,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it introduces MeZO,</font>

29
00:02:10,088 --> 00:02:13,958
<font color="#FFFFFF">a memory-efficient zeroth-order optimizer that
adapts classical zeroth-order SGD to operate in place,</font>

30
00:02:14,038 --> 00:02:17,907
<font color="#FFFFFF">so fine-tuning costs no more memory than inference.</font>

31
00:02:17,987 --> 00:02:21,856
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, through comprehensive experiments across
masked and autoregressive models,</font>

32
00:02:21,936 --> 00:02:25,805
<font color="#FFFFFF">scales up to sixty-six billion parameters, and
classification, multiple-choice,</font>

33
00:02:25,885 --> 00:02:29,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">and generation tasks,</font>

34
00:02:29,835 --> 00:02:33,704
<font color="#FFFFFF">it shows MeZO matches full backpropagation
fine-tuning while using far less memory.</font>

35
00:02:33,784 --> 00:02:37,653
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

36
00:02:37,733 --> 00:02:41,602
<font color="#FFFFFF">it demonstrates compatibility with
parameter-efficient methods like LoRA and prefix tuning,</font>

37
00:02:41,682 --> 00:02:45,552
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the ability to optimize non-differentiable
objectives such as accuracy or F1.</font>

38
00:02:45,632 --> 00:02:49,501
<font color="#FFFFFF">Fourth,</font>

39
00:02:49,581 --> 00:02:53,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">it supplies theory explaining why MeZO converges
quickly despite the enormous parameter count.</font>

40
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:58,493
<font color="#FFFFFF">MeZO is built on a classical zeroth-order gradient
estimator called SPSA,</font>

41
00:02:58,573 --> 00:03:02,001
<font color="#FFFFFF">or simultaneous perturbation stochastic approximation.</font>

42
00:03:02,081 --> 00:03:05,964
<font color="#FFFFFF">At each step it samples a single random Gaussian
direction z,</font>

43
00:03:06,044 --> 00:03:09,928
<font color="#FFFFFF">adds epsilon times z to every parameter and records
the loss,</font>

44
00:03:10,008 --> 00:03:13,306
<font color="#FFFFFF">then subtracts to get the loss on the opposite side.</font>

45
00:03:13,386 --> 00:03:20,128
<font color="#FFFFFF">The difference of these two losses divided by twice
epsilon gives a scalar called the projected gradient,</font>

46
00:03:20,208 --> 00:03:26,495
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the parameters are then updated by moving along
z scaled by this scalar and the learning rate.</font>

47
00:03:26,575 --> 00:03:31,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">The crucial engineering insight is that instead of
storing the full random vector z,</font>

48
00:03:32,032 --> 00:03:33,642
<font color="#FFFFFF">which would double memory,</font>

49
00:03:33,722 --> 00:03:39,619
<font color="#FFFFFF">MeZO stores only the random seed and regenerates z
deterministically each time it is needed.</font>

50
00:03:39,699 --> 00:03:44,362
<font color="#FFFFFF">This lets the entire perturb, evaluate, and update
cycle happen in place,</font>

51
00:03:44,442 --> 00:03:47,870
<font color="#FFFFFF">so training uses exactly the same memory as inference.</font>

52
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:50,673
<font color="#FFFFFF">The evaluation is deliberately broad.</font>

53
00:03:50,753 --> 00:03:52,973
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the masked-language-model side,</font>

54
00:03:53,053 --> 00:03:57,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">it uses RoBERTa-large on six
sentence-classification and inference tasks,</font>

55
00:03:57,992 --> 00:04:05,828
<font color="#FFFFFF">tested in both a few-shot regime with sixteen
examples per class and a many-shot regime with five hundred and twelve.</font>

56
00:04:05,908 --> 00:04:07,654
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the autoregressive side,</font>

57
00:04:07,734 --> 00:04:13,811
<font color="#FFFFFF">it uses OPT models ranging from one-point-three
billion up to sixty-six billion parameters,</font>

58
00:04:13,891 --> 00:04:16,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">evaluated on SuperGLUE classification tasks,</font>

59
00:04:16,868 --> 00:04:19,697
<font color="#FFFFFF">multiple-choice tasks like COPA and ReCoRD,</font>

60
00:04:19,777 --> 00:04:22,809
<font color="#FFFFFF">and generation tasks including SQuAD and DROP.</font>

61
00:04:22,889 --> 00:04:27,950
<font color="#FFFFFF">Baselines include zero-shot prediction, in-context
learning, linear probing,</font>

62
00:04:28,030 --> 00:04:29,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">and standard Adam fine-tuning.</font>

63
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:37,771
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is that MeZO closes most of the
gap to backpropagation fine-tuning at a fraction of the memory.</font>

64
00:04:37,851 --> 00:04:39,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">On OPT-thirteen-billion,</font>

65
00:04:39,414 --> 00:04:44,936
<font color="#FFFFFF">MeZO comes within about one percent of full Adam
fine-tuning on seven of eleven tasks,</font>

66
00:04:45,016 --> 00:04:47,997
<font color="#FFFFFF">while consuming only one-twelfth of the memory,</font>

67
00:04:48,077 --> 00:04:53,403
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it clearly beats zero-shot prediction,
in-context learning, and linear probing.</font>

68
00:04:53,483 --> 00:04:56,139
<font color="#FFFFFF">On RoBERTa-large in the many-shot setting,</font>

69
00:04:56,219 --> 00:04:59,526
<font color="#FFFFFF">it lands within roughly five percent of fine-tuning.</font>

70
00:04:59,606 --> 00:05:02,587
<font color="#FFFFFF">Beyond memory, MeZO is also faster in practice,</font>

71
00:05:02,667 --> 00:05:08,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">requiring about half the GPU-hours of Adam
fine-tuning for a thirty-billion-parameter model.</font>

72
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:11,923
<font color="#FFFFFF">Several ablations probe MeZO's flexibility.</font>

73
00:05:12,003 --> 00:05:17,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">Combining MeZO with parameter-efficient methods,
namely LoRA and prefix tuning,</font>

74
00:05:17,595 --> 00:05:20,983
<font color="#FFFFFF">gives accuracy on par with tuning all parameters,</font>

75
00:05:21,063 --> 00:05:23,814
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing the two approaches compose well.</font>

76
00:05:23,894 --> 00:05:29,122
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using a single random perturbation per step, rather
than averaging several,</font>

77
00:05:29,202 --> 00:05:34,785
<font color="#FFFFFF">turns out to be the most efficient setting for a
fixed number of forward passes.</font>

78
00:05:34,865 --> 00:05:39,244
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because MeZO only needs loss values and never
actual gradients,</font>

79
00:05:39,324 --> 00:05:43,136
<font color="#FFFFFF">it can optimize non-differentiable objectives directly,</font>

80
00:05:43,216 --> 00:05:45,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">such as maximizing accuracy or F1 score.</font>

81
00:05:46,048 --> 00:05:49,436
<font color="#FFFFFF">And against a prior zeroth-order baseline, BBTv2,</font>

82
00:05:49,516 --> 00:05:53,470
<font color="#FFFFFF">MeZO improves accuracy by up to eleven percentage points.</font>

83
00:05:53,850 --> 00:05:55,955
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

84
00:05:56,035 --> 00:06:03,108
<font color="#FFFFFF">MeZO delivers up to a twelvefold reduction in
memory compared with Adam fine-tuning on OPT-thirteen-billion,</font>

85
00:06:03,188 --> 00:06:05,492
<font color="#FFFFFF">using no more memory than inference.</font>

86
00:06:05,572 --> 00:06:07,677
<font color="#FFFFFF">On a single eighty-gigabyte A100,</font>

87
00:06:07,757 --> 00:06:14,234
<font color="#FFFFFF">it trains a thirty-billion-parameter model where
backpropagation fits only two-point-seven billion.</font>

88
00:06:14,314 --> 00:06:19,068
<font color="#FFFFFF">It matches fine-tuning within about one percent on
seven of eleven tasks,</font>

89
00:06:19,148 --> 00:06:23,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it roughly halves the GPU-hours needed at the
thirty-billion scale.</font>

90
00:06:24,150 --> 00:06:29,258
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting takeaway is that fine-tuning no longer
strictly requires backpropagation.</font>

91
00:06:29,338 --> 00:06:34,019
<font color="#FFFFFF">With MeZO, you can adapt very large language models
using only forward passes,</font>

92
00:06:34,099 --> 00:06:35,972
<font color="#FFFFFF">at the memory cost of inference,</font>

93
00:06:36,052 --> 00:06:40,366
<font color="#FFFFFF">and still match the quality of gradient-based
fine-tuning on many tasks.</font>

94
00:06:40,446 --> 00:06:45,737
<font color="#FFFFFF">This overturns the classical worry that
zeroth-order methods must scale badly with model</font>

95
00:06:45,817 --> 00:06:48,972
<font color="#FFFFFF">size: when you start from a strong pre-trained model,</font>

96
00:06:49,052 --> 00:06:54,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">convergence is governed by the effective local
structure of the loss landscape rather</font>

97
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:56,357
<font color="#FFFFFF">than the sheer number of parameters,</font>

98
00:06:56,437 --> 00:06:59,470
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is why MeZO works at billion-parameter scale.</font>
